{
 "name": "indoor_occlusion_gather",
 "saveFile": "minimal_colony.rws",
 "description": "Indoor sky occlusion's gather phase is a change to WHEN and WHERE the window fill happens, never to what it produces, so the only acceptable result is identical frames between occl_gather_on.png and occl_gather_off.png. Measured: 0 of 2,073,600 pixels differ, median CIELAB dE 0.0000. A visible difference is a bug, not a trade-off. WHY THE COUNTERS ARE PINNED BESIDE THE FRAMES: the phase's failure mode is that it silently stops matching -- the candidate predicate drifts from Section.TryUpdate's, or another mod patches the glow accessors and SkyOcclusionGather.ParallelSafe closes the gate -- after which every section falls back to the inline build, the pixels stay correct, the cost stays what it was, and the frame comparison reads a confident zero. Two identical frames prove the phase is HARMLESS; only occl_gather_passes being non-zero proves it RAN. WHERE THE RESET GOES, and it is the trap this file already fell into once: the harness runs ONE STEP PER FRAME and every occlusion-family SetFeature re-runs ForceRebuild, so zeroing the counters BEFORE the flag block counts rebakes made while the previous arm's flag was still live -- the off arm reported 4 passes and 448 hits that way, which looks exactly like the flag not working. Reset after the flags have settled, then provoke one clean rebake by writing a flag the value it already has. The cloud lanes are stated off explicitly: the sheet drifts on the tick counter, so leaving them on would put run-to-run noise on exactly the outdoor terrain the two frames are differenced over, and SetWeather Clear does not turn them off because partial cover during Clear is our own feature. THE CAMERA IS RE-AIMED BEFORE EVERY CAPTURE: it eases toward a LookAt target rather than snapping, so arms of different lengths photograph from slightly different positions, and a whole-frame translation reads as a large lighting difference (this file measured median dE 2.75 that way before the fix) rather than as the camera artefact it is.",
